Copilot code review is now available to the <%= @org_name %> organization

Members of the <%= @org_name %>  organization with a GitHub Copilot subscription now have access to GitHub Copilot code review (public preview). There are three ways to get a review from Copilot:

  - Tag Copilot as a pull request reviewer on GitHub.com from the "Reviewers" menu: <%= Copilot::CODE_REVIEW_REQUEST_DOCS_URL %>
  - Enable automatic reviews for new pull requests on GitHub.com using rulesets (admin permissions required): <%= Copilot::CODE_REVIEW_AUTOMATIC_DOCS_URL %>
  - Use the "Review Changes" button in Visual Studio Code", available when drafting a commit in the "Source Control" tab: <%= Copilot::CODE_REVIEW_VS_CODE_DOCS_URL %>

To get the most out of Copilot code review, we recommend that you enable automatic reviews for your repositories on GitHub.com. For more information on Copilot code review, head to the <%= link_to "docs", Copilot::CODE_REVIEW_DOCS_URL %>.

<% if @has_enterprise_access %>
Support for custom coding guidelines, allowing you to tailor Copilot's reviews to your codebase, is coming soon.
<% end %>

Taking Copilot's suggestions forward with Copilot Workspace on GitHub.com

Alongside Copilot code review, your users also have access to Copilot Workspace in the context of their pull requests, a new experience on GitHub.com where they can validate and tweak Copilot's suggestions before applying them to a branch.

To jump into Workspace, just click the "Open in Workspace" on a suggestion from Copilot code review on GitHub.com, or use the "Edit" button on any file. To learn more about Copilot Workspace, head to the docs: <%= Copilot::WORKSPACE_FOR_PRS_DOCS_URL %>.

Getting supporting and sharing your feedback

We'd love to hear feedback from you and your team so we can make Copilot code review and Copilot Workspace even better.

When you receive a comment from Copilot, you can give feedback directly with the 👍 and 👎 buttons.

